<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Hungary (1867-1918)</h1><p class="st-meta">1867 CE – 1918 CE · capital Budapest</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Self-governing Hungarian half of Austria-Hungary, a time of rapid growth and Budapest's rise, and of tension with the kingdom's many nationalities.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria's</a> defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1866 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> to settle with the Hungarian leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferenc-deak">Ferenc Deák</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyula-andrassy">Gyula Andrássy</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a> restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary's</a> constitution and parliament, with the emperor crowned king, and created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, sharing only the monarch, foreign policy, the army and common finances. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-hungarian-settlement">Croatian-Hungarian Settlement</a> of 1868 gave <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-slavonia">Croatia-Slavonia</a> autonomy within it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Parliament was dominated by the landed nobility and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> under a narrow franchise, led by premiers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalman-tisza">Kálmán Tisza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istvan-tisza">István Tisza</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pest-hungary">Pest</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obuda">Óbuda</a> merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> in 1873, which grew into a major industrial city, celebrated in the Millennium of 1896. Policies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyarisation">Magyarisation</a>, such as the Apponyi school laws of 1907, angered Slovaks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aster-revolution">Aster Revolution</a> of October 1918, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hungarian-democratic-republic">Hungarian Democratic Republic</a> was proclaimed in November. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> in 1920 transferred two-thirds of the former kingdom to its neighbours.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-hungary-1867-1918.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Hungary (1867-1918)" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hungary_(1915-1918;_angels).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1867 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Budapest</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Franz Joseph I, Charles IV, Gyula Andrássy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic and Protestant Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hungarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Hungary, Slovakia, Romania, Croatia, Serbia, Ukraine</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Transleithania, Lands of the Crown of Saint Stephen</dd></dl></aside></div>
